satellite imagery through,

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.810
and we'll return back the
top 500 most similar images

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.574
to what we just clicked on.

00:09:47.574 --> 00:09:49.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I think
it is pretty interesting

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.080
because we were previously
talking with Kalev,

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:56.400
and he was mentioning how he
uses, for his project details,

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.060
he uses Cloud Vision and--

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:01.490
but Cloud Vision is
just like a REST API

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:03.060
and you can very easily use it.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:05.670
If you want to dig
deeper, you can definitely

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:09.027
change, basically, the
engine of how it works.

00:10:09.027 --> 00:10:10.110
Is that what you're doing?

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.455
Are you running Cloud ML?

00:10:11.455 --> 00:10:12.580
Are you running TensorFlow?

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:13.980
Tell us a little bit about it.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.230
TIM KELTON: Yeah, these
are TensorFlow models.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:19.470
And basically, we
extract a feature vector

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.510
for each of those
little tiny scenes

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:23.130
that we see all over the Earth.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:26.210
And that has a unique signature
and then we rank them,

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:29.400
and we do a similarity in
which other features anywhere

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.650
else on the earth are similar.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.260
So it's something you
could never scale--

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.720
you could never
hire 1,000 people

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.590
to just rank every single
pixel all over the world

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:42.600
and say, how similar is
this to this, you know?

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.180
You have to automate it
enough to use something

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.472
like TensorFlow.

00:10:46.472 --> 00:10:48.180
MARK MANDEL: And I
saw on the demo that--

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:51.030
the demo, the actual app
that you can scale in and out

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:53.290
visually from like
America or China

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:54.832
or basically sort
of other nations.

00:10:54.832 --> 00:10:57.040
I believe you're using Google
Cloud Storage for that.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.110
Is that-- what's
happening there?

00:10:58.110 --> 00:10:59.484
TIM KELTON: Yeah,
under the hood,

00:10:59.484 --> 00:11:02.430
we could never attach enough
PD disks to a single VM

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.570
to store all of that imagery.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.470
So we had to have
another solution.

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:10.350
So we're actually storing all
of the compressed and tiled

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.890
imagery in Google Cloud
Storage, and then we

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:17.040
have a layer on top of
that called Festivus.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.250
And that's a file system layer
on top of object storage,

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:23.730
and that allows us
system-level access

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:26.217
just as if it was native disk.

00:11:26.217 --> 00:11:28.800
And that's really we can only
do that because cloud storage is

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.560
just so fast returning
back the objects.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:32.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:35.080
I'm actually curious
about two things.

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.475
First, is Festivus--

00:11:36.475 --> 00:11:38.850
I don't know how to pronounce
it correctly, but Festivus,

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:40.759
is that open source
by any chance?

00:11:40.759 --> 00:11:43.050
TIM KELTON: No, it's just
something we built. Actually,

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:47.070
our CTO wrote all the code
over a weekend, pretty much,

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.870
and got it all to
run really well.

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:51.870
And it's just been this really
core part of our infrastructure

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.730
to getting it to
scale, and we're still

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:58.244
a pretty small company focused
on building products right now.

00:11:58.244 --> 00:11:59.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:02.220
And the second one was, how
are you actually managing this?

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.950
You said there was
petabytes of data in use.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:06.830
I mean, and it just works?

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:08.330
Like is there some
magic going on,

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:11.160
or it's just because
cloud storage is fast?

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:12.650
TIM KELTON: Well,
under the hood,

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:15.390
you know, we're storing lots--
we have a fairly sophisticated

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:19.620
processing pipeline to get all
the images in and have them all

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.840
correct and be really
uniform and have

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:24.030
a real nice API in front of it.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:28.350
But then, in front of that, to
serve it, we have GKE clusters

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.270
and managed instance
group clusters

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.310
that we horizontally scale.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:34.590
And then you have front
end load balancers

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:37.770
that are in front of those
instance groups and GKE

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:38.271
clusters.

00:12:38.271 --> 00:12:39.811
MARK MANDEL: Sounds
like something we

00:12:39.811 --> 00:12:42.062
